<section xml:id="section-rings-definition">
  <title>The Definition of a Ring</title>
  <p>
    We started the course talking about groups. Groups are 
    fundamental to  abstract mathematics and we've now worked
    with many interesting examples. But a group is far from the only
    structure defined in abstract algebra. We're moving on to the next
    structure: a ring. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Before we get to the definition, the main idea behind a ring is
    the presence of two binary operations which interact. A group only
    had one operation, at least by definition. But many mathematical
    structures have two, often some kind of addition and some kind of
    multiplication. A ring is the way to try to understand these
    structure. Let me move on to the formal definition.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A set <m>R</m> is a <term>ring</term> if it satisfies the
        following properties. 
        <ul>
          <li>
            There is a binary operation, written as additively by
            default, such that <m>(R,+)</m> is an abelian group. 
          </li>
          <li>
            There is a second associative binary operation, written
            multiplicatively by default. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The distributive law holds for the two operations. That
            is, if <m>a,b,c \in R</m>, the following equations are
            always true. (I have to state both because multiplication
            may not be commutative.) 
            <md>
              <mrow>
                a(b+c) = ab + ac 
              </mrow>
              <mrow>
                (b+c)a = ba + ca 
              </mrow>
            </md>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <example>
    <p>
      I must start with the most important example: the integers. In
      many ways, the integers are the archetypical ring. They are an
      abelian group under addition. Multiplication is also defined,
      but it is not a group structure. This is important: in a ring,
      the multiplication can be like the integers, where inverses
      mostly don't exist. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    I'll keep returning to the integers throughout this section.
    Many of the structures of ring theory arise directly from
    structure of the integers. They really are the start of ring
    theory. For now, though, let me give some other definitions
    relating to the properties of the second binary operation in a
    ring. 
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>R</m> be a ring.
        <ul>
          <li>
            The second binary operation does not need to be
            commutative. If it is commutative, then the ring is called
            a <term>commutative ring</term>. (Note that the term
            <sq>abelian</sq> is not normally used for rings. For
            whatever reason, that terms is exclusive to group theory.
            Rings are just commutative.) 
          </li>
          <li>
            The second binary operation does not need to have an
            identity element. If it does, then the ring is called a
            <term>ring with identity</term>. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The second binary operation, even if there is an identity,
            does not have to have inverses. If a ring with identity
            has inverses for all elements <em>except for the
            additive identity</em>, then it is called a
            <term>division ring</term>. 
          </li>
          <li>
            A commutative division ring is called a
            <term>field</term>. 
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    Note that the division ring definition excludes the additive
    identity, usually written at <m>0</m>. In ring theory, just like
    in normal number systems, you can never divide by zero.
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      The integers are a commutative ring with identity. The integers
      are not a division ring. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      <m>\QQ</m>, <m>\RR</m> and <m>\CC</m> are all fields: they all
      have commutative multiplication and all non-zero elements have
      multiplicative inverses (reciprocals). 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Let <m>p</m> be a prime number. <m>\ZZ/p\ZZ</m> is a field. It
      has a commutative multiplication and all non-zero elements are
      invertible. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Let <m>n</m> be a compositve number. <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ</m> is
      commutative ring with identity, but it is not a division ring.
      Any element that shares a factor with <m>n</m> is not
      multiplicatively invertible. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      <m>\RR[x]</m> is a commutative ring with identity. I can add and
      multiply polynomials. All polynomials have additive inverse,
      <m>-p(x)</m>, but no polynomials have multiplicative inverses. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example xml:id="example-poly-ring">
    <p>
      In fact, if <m>R</m> is any commutative ring, then I can make
      polynomials <m>R[x]</m> in the variable <m>x</m> over that ring.
      The coefficients will be elements of <m>R</m>, whatever they
      are. <m>R[x]</m> will also be a commutative ring, since it will
      inherit the commutativity of the coefficients. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      <m>2\ZZ</m> is the set of even integers. This is a commutative
      ring without identity, since <m>1</m> is not an even integer.
      Even though there is no multiplicative inverses, the ring
      conditions are still satisfied. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      I defined the quaternions, <m>Q</m>, in <xref
      ref="definition-quaternions"/>. Using the quaternions, I can make
      the <term>real Hamiltonian quaternion ring</term> as follows. 
      <me>
        \HH = \{a + bi + cj + dk | a,b,c,d \in \RR\}
      </me> 
      The addition is component-wise, very much like with polynomials. 
      <md>
        <mrow>
          (a_1 + b_1i + c_1j + d_1k) + (a_2 + b_2i + c_2j + d_1k) = 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          (a_1 + a_1) + (b_1 + b_2)i + (c_1 + c_2)j+ (d_1 + d_2)k 
        </mrow>
      </md> 
      This defined an abelian addition. The multiplication happens by
      distributing out all the elements and using the rules of the
      quaternion group or reduce all of the products down to <m>i</m>,
      <m>j</m> and <m>k</m>. 
    </p>
    <p>
      The result of this setup is a non-commutative ring. The
      multiplication of the quaternion group is non-commutative, and
      that non-commutativity is now inherited by this ring. This is
      one of the most accessible examples of a non-commutative ring.
      In this course, we will almost exclusively deal with commutative
      rings, but the algebra of non-commutative rings is a fascinating
      piece of mathematics itself. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Many function groups are also rings. <m>C(\RR)</m>,
      <m>C^1(\RR)</m> and <m>C^\infty(\RR)</m> are all rings with
      pointwise addition and multiplication. They are all commutative
      rings, but not division rings. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Let <m>F</m> be the set of nowhere-zero continuous functions
      <m>f: \RR \rightarrow \RR^*</m>, along with the zero function.
      <sq>Nowhere-zero</sq> means that the output of the function
      cannot be zero at any point; the codomain also shows this. This
      is a commutative division ring under pointwise addition and
      multiplication. This ring is a divsion ring because of the
      nowhere-zero condition: any function <m>f</m> that never outputs
      zero has a reciprocal <m>\frac{1}{f}</m> which is well-defined
      on the same domain.  This reciprocal is the multiplicative
      inverse. There is no multiplicative inverse for the zero
      function, but that's fine in ring theory: we never need or want
      to divide by the appropriate <sq>zero</sq> in any ring. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      A rational function is a ratio of two polynomials: <m>f(x) =
      \frac{p(x)}{q(x)}</m>. The set of all rational functions with
      real coefficients is written <m>\RR(x)</m>. This is a field
      under the addition and multiplication of polynomials and the
      rules of fractions. Since we allow polynomials in the
      denominator, any rational function which is not the zero
      function has a reciprocal, which is its multiplicative inverse.
      The domains of these functions vary depending on the zeros of
      the denominator, but that's fine; this ring structure is
      unconcerned about the specific domains. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      Let <m>M_n(\RR)</m> be the set of all <m>n \times n</m> matrices
      with real coefficients. This is a non-commuatitive ring with
      identity under the operations of matrix addition and matrix
      multiplication. (Matrix addition is componentwise: each
      component of the matrix is added to the appropriate component of
      the other matrix. The zero matrix is the additive inverse.)
      Matrix addition may be unfamiliar, but you can prove that the
      distributive law does, in fact, work for these operations. 
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a ring with identity because of the identity matrix. It
      is not a commutative ring because matrix multiplication is not
      commutative. It is not a division ring because not all matrices
      are invertible. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    Now we have a bunch of rings to talk about. You may have noticed
    that, compared with groups theory, almost all of these are
    infinite sets. This is an important observation. Our investigation
    of group theory primarily focused on finite groups and, indeed,
    finite non-abelian groups are fascinating. Finite rings, though
    useful, are not as rich. As groups, they are all abelian, so just
    products of groups isomorphic to <m>\ZZ/p^r\ZZ</m>. It is
    worthwhile to know the rings structurs on these sets, but they
    are only a tiny piece of ring theory. We will mostly be concerned
    with infinite rings. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Let me now state some of the first properties of rings. These are
    all algebraic tricks that we are very used to using for normal
    numbers. It's very nice to know that they still work for general
    rings. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>R</m> be a ring and let <m>a,b, \in R</m>. Write
        <m>0</m> for the additive identity and write <m>-a</m> for the
        additive inverse of <m>a</m>.
        <ul>
          <li>
            <m>0a = a0 = 0</m>
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>(-a)b = a(-b) = -(ab)</m>
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>(-a)(-b) = ab</m>
          </li>
          <li>
            If this is a ring with identity and the multiplicative
            identity is written <m>1</m>, then <m>-a = (-1)a</m>. 
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Here is a calculation.
            <me>
              0 + 0a = 0a = (0 + 0)a = 0a + 0a 
            </me>
            By cancelation (which I know I can do in the abelian
            group), I can conclude <m>0 = 0a</m>, finishing this part
            of the proof. 
          </li>
          <li>
            Again, here is a calculation.
            <me>
              (-a)b + ab = (-a + a)b = 0 b = 0 
            </me>
            This proves that <m>(-a)b</m> is the additive inverse to
            <m>ab</m>, which means it must equal <m>-(ab)</m>. A
            similar calcluation works for <m>a(-b)</m><mdash/>please
            feel free to verify for yourself. 
          </li>
          <li>
            I can use the previous result twice. 
            <me>
              (-a)(-b) = -(a)(-b) = -(-(ab)) = ab
            </me>
            This last step is true since, in any group, taking an
            inverse twice returns the original element. 
          </li>
          <li>
            Again, I can calculate. 
            <me>
              (-1)(a) + a = (-1) a + (1) a = (-1 + 1)a = 0a = 0 
            </me>
            This show that <m>(-1)(a)</m> is the additive inverse to
            <m>a</m>, so <m>(-1)(a) = - a</m>. 
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    I've often said that mathematicians love their trivial cases, so
    let me use a proposition to talk about more example.
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>R</m> be a ring with identity where the same element is
        both the additive and multiplicative inverse. (That is, where
        <m>0 = 1</m>.) Then this ring has only one element and is
        called the <term>trivial ring</term> or the <term>zero
        ring</term>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Writing <m>0</m> for the additive inverse and <m>1</m> for the
        multiplicative inverse, assume that <m>0=1</m>. Then let
        <m>a \in R</m> be any ring element. I can make this very short
        calculation.
        <me>
          a = 1a = 0a = 0 
        </me>
        This calculate shows that all the ring elements are equal to
        0. Therefore, the ring must only be <m>\{0\}</m>. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    It's going to be a bit annoying to always state <m>1 \neq 0</m> in
    every defition. From now on, in all discussion about rings, I will
    simply assume that the additive and multiplicative identity are
    not the same. When I want to talk about this trivial ring, I'll
    mention it explicitly. (Some historical definitions of a ring
    include <m>1 \neq 0</m> as part of the definition to avoid this
    entirely.)
  </p>
  <p>
    I have some more fundamental ring theory definitions and
    properties to cover before I finish this section. Let me talk
    about the elements of a ring. 
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>R</m> be a ring. As usual, we use <sq>zero</sq> to
        refer to the additive inverse in the ring.
        <ul>
          <li>
            A non-zero element of the ring is called a
            <term>unit</term> if is has a multiplicative inverse. The
            set of all unit is written <m>R^*</m>. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The units in a ring with identity always form a group under
            multiplication. This is called the <term>unit group</term>
            of the ring. 
          </li>
          <li>
            A non-zero element of the ring <m>a</m> is called a
            <term>zero-divisor</term> if there is another non-zero
            element <m>b</m> such that <m>ab = 0</m>. (For
            non-commutative rings, this is technically a left-zero
            divisor, but I'll ignore that subtlety in these notes.) 
          </li>
          <li>
            A ring with no zero divisors if called an <term>integral
            domain</term> or often more succintly, a <term>domain</term>. 
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    This notation for units matches what we did in group theory, where
    <m>\RR^*</m>, <m>\QQ^*</m> and <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ^*</m> were all groups
    under multiplication. The units are precisely those elements which
    are invertible, so they do form unit groups. We have now
    generalized this star-notation to any ring. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Let me demonstrate more about the definitions though some examples. 
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Consider <m>\ZZ/10\ZZ</m>. The units in this ring are 1, 3, 7
      and 9, which we already new studying the group
      <m>\ZZ/10\ZZ^*</m>. The zero divisors are 2, 4, 5, 6, and 8. I
      can demonstrate this explicity. 
      <md>
        <mrow>
          2 \cdot 5 = 0 \mod 10 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          4 \cdot 5 = 0 \mod 10 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          6 \cdot 5 = 0 \mod 10 
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          8 \cdot 5 = 0 \mod 10 
        </mrow>
      </md>
      This calculation shows all five of these elemenets are zero
      divisors. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Generalizing this leads to the following observation: in
      <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ</m>, non-zero elements are either units or zero
      divisors. The units represented by numbers coprime to <m>n</m>,
      and the zero-divisors by numbers which share a factor with
      <m>n</m>. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      The units in <m>\ZZ</m> are <m>1</m> and <m>-1</m>. There are no
      units in <m>2\ZZ</m>. (In general, there are no units in any
      ring without identity, since an identity is required to define
      multiplicative inverses.) 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      The units in <m>M_n(\RR)</m> are exactly the group <m>GL(n)</m>
      of invertible matrices. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      In a field or a division ring, everything other than zero has a
      multiplicative inverse. Therefore, the unit groups <m>\RR^*</m>,
      <m>\QQ^*</m>, <m>\CC^*</m>, <m>\ZZ/p\ZZ^*</m>, <m>\HH^*</m> and
      <m>\RR(x)^*</m> are all elements other than zero. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <example>
    <p>
      The units in <m>\RR[x]</m> are just <m>\RR^*</m>. No
      non-constant polynomial has a multiplicative inverses, so only
      the non-zero constants can be inverted. 
    </p>
  </example>
  <p>
    Groups had subgroups. The same general idea works for rings. 
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>R</m> be a ring. A subset <m>S \subset R</m> is a
        <term>subring</term> if <m>S</m> is also a ring the same two
        binary operations as <m>R</m>. This means, in particular, that
        <m>S</m> is closed under both operations. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    <m>\ZZ</m> is a subring of <m>\QQ</m>, which is a subring of
    <m>\RR</m>.  Subrings can have different properties than the whole
    ring. For example, <m>2\ZZ</m> is a subring of <m>\ZZ</m>, but it
    lacks identity. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Subgroups, and particularly normal subgroups, are completely
    central to group theory. It turns out that subrings are much less
    important to ring theory. In a later section, I'll define a new
    subobject which will play the role of a normal subgroup in ring
    theory. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, here are two more useful properties for the algebra of
    rings. I'll state these without proof. 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>R</m> be a ring and let <m>a,b,c \in R</m> be non-zero
        elements. 
        <ul>
          <li>
            Multiplicative cancellation only works with units. That
            is, if <m>c</m> is a unit, then the following implication
            is true. 
            <me>
              ac = bc \implies a = b 
            </me>
            A similar statement is true for multiplication on the
            left. In a group, cancellation was always possible. Now I
            have to be careful and only cancel when the cancelled
            element is a unit. 
          </li>
          <li>
            Most exponent laws still hold. However, like with groups,
            I can only distribute exponents if the multiplication is
            commutative. 
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </proposition>
</section>
